from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Besides Portugal and Brazil, Portuguese is an official language in several countries of…","options":["North America","Africa","the Arctic","Central Asia"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Angola, Mozambique, Cape Verde, Guinea-Bissau, and Sao Tome and Principe are all Lusophone African nations - see ''The Lusophone world''."},{"prompt":"The navigator who first reached India by sea, in 1498, was…","options":["Christopher Columbus","Vasco da Gama","Ferdinand Magellan","Henry the Navigator"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Vasco da Gama opened the sea route from Europe to India around Africa - see ''The Age of Discovery''."},{"prompt":"The Portuguese national epic The Lusiads was written by…","options":["Fernando Pessoa","Machado de Assis","Luis de Camoes","Jorge Amado"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Camoes published Os Lusiadas in 1572, celebrating da Gama''s voyage - see ''Literature''."},{"prompt":"Fado is best described as a…","options":["Brazilian carnival drumming style","melancholic Portuguese urban song","religious chant from Fatima","regional folk dance of Porto"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fado is a mournful song tradition tied to Lisbon and Coimbra - see ''Music''."},{"prompt":"Azulejos are…","options":["salted codfish dishes","painted glazed ceramic tiles","carnival costumes","fishing boats"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Azulejos are the decorative tiles that cover Portuguese churches, palaces, and stations - see ''Art and azulejos''."},{"prompt":"Pedro Alvares Cabral is credited with reaching which land in 1500?","options":["India","Brazil","Japan","Australia"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cabral landed on the Brazilian coast in 1500, claiming it for Portugal - see ''The Age of Discovery''."},{"prompt":"Bossa nova emerged in the late 1950s in…","options":["Portugal","Angola","Brazil","Cape Verde"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Bossa nova grew out of samba and jazz in Rio de Janeiro - see ''Music''."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'The Lusophone world: Portugal, Brazil, and Lusophone Africa', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Name the main Portuguese-speaking countries across three continents, explain what ''Lusophone'' means, and describe how a single language came to link roughly 260 million people. Warm-up: besides Portugal itself, which country do you think has the most Portuguese speakers? Hold that guess - the answer reshapes how most newcomers picture the Portuguese-speaking world, because the old colonial center is now far from its largest voice. By the end of this lesson you should be able to sketch the Lusophone map from memory and say why it looks the way it does."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The word ''Lusophone'' comes from Lusitania, the Roman name for the territory that is now Portugal; a Lusophone country is one where Portuguese is an official or widely spoken language. The language spread outward during Portugal''s age of maritime expansion, so today it anchors a community of nations on four continents. Portugal sits in Europe, on the western edge of the Iberian Peninsula. Brazil, in South America, is by far the largest, home to more than 200 million people - over four fifths of all native speakers. In Africa the Lusophone states are Angola, Mozambique, Cape Verde, Guinea-Bissau, and Sao Tome and Principe, often grouped together as the PALOP. In Asia, East Timor keeps Portuguese as a co-official language, and the former territory of Macau retains Portuguese traces in its street signs and law. These states cooperate through the Community of Portuguese Language Countries, the CPLP, founded in 1996 to promote their shared language and heritage. One tongue thus holds together very different histories, climates, and cuisines in a loose but genuine kinship."},{"heading":"A tour of the Lusophone map","text":"Portugal, the historic center, is geographically small but gave the language its name and its oldest literature. Brazil, independent since 1822, turned Portuguese into a global giant and reshaped it with Indigenous and African words such as ''abacaxi'' for pineapple and ''samba'' for its most famous music. Angola and Mozambique, both independent in 1975, are the largest African members; Angola''s capital, Luanda, is now one of the biggest Portuguese-speaking cities anywhere. Cape Verde, an island nation off West Africa, blends Portuguese with a Creole called Kriolu and gave the world the singer Cesaria Evora. Guinea-Bissau and Sao Tome and Principe are smaller but no less Lusophone. East Timor, which secured independence in 2002, embraced Portuguese partly as a badge of identity distinct from its larger neighbors. Across this map, accents, slang, and even spelling differ, yet a reader in Lisbon can still follow a newspaper from Maputo or Sao Paulo. The 1990 Orthographic Agreement tried to standardize spelling across these countries, with uneven enthusiasm. The Lusophone world is therefore plural: not one culture but many cultures speaking in overlapping voices."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What does ''Lusophone'' mean, and where does the word come from? 2) Which Lusophone country has the most speakers? 3) Name three Lusophone countries in Africa. 4) What does CPLP stand for? 5) Which Asian country uses Portuguese as a co-official language? 6) Roughly how many people speak Portuguese worldwide? Answers: 1) Portuguese-speaking, from Lusitania, the Roman name for Portugal. 2) Brazil, with more than 200 million. 3) Any three of Angola, Mozambique, Cape Verde, Guinea-Bissau, and Sao Tome and Principe. 4) The Community of Portuguese Language Countries. 5) East Timor. 6) About 260 million, the great majority of them in Brazil."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming Portugal has the most Portuguese speakers - Brazil dwarfs it many times over. Mistake: treating ''Lusophone'' as a single culture rather than a family of distinct nations with their own foods, music, and dialects. Mistake: forgetting the African members entirely, even though Luanda and Maputo are major Portuguese-speaking cities. Mistake: imagining the language stopped spreading after the colonial era, when CPLP cooperation keeps it a living, shared project. Exit: (a) On how many continents is Portuguese an official language? (b) In what year was the CPLP founded? Answers: Four - Europe, South America, Africa, and Asia; the CPLP was founded in 1996."}]}'::jsonb, '''Lusophone'' means Portuguese-speaking, from Lusitania, the Roman name for the land that became Portugal.', 12),
  (3, 'The Age of Discovery: Henry, da Gama, and Cabral', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Place the main Portuguese voyages of the 1400s and 1500s in order, name the figures behind them, and explain why a small Atlantic kingdom briefly led the world in ocean navigation. Warm-up: what might push a country with little farmland and a long coastline to look to the sea for wealth and power? Keep that question in mind - geography and ambition together explain much of what follows, and they set up the colonial story that shapes Brazil and Lusophone Africa to this day."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"In the 1400s Portugal launched a sustained program of ocean exploration, seeking a sea route to the spice markets of Asia that would bypass the overland trade controlled by others. Prince Henry, remembered as Henry the Navigator, never sailed far himself but sponsored expeditions down the West African coast and gathered pilots, mapmakers, and shipwrights around the southern town of Sagres. His captains pushed past capes that sailors had long feared. The breakthrough caravel, a nimble ship that could sail against the wind, made longer voyages possible. Decade by decade the Portuguese charted the African shore, set up trading posts, and entered the trade in gold, ivory, and, tragically, enslaved people. By 1488 Bartolomeu Dias had rounded the southern tip of Africa, the Cape of Good Hope, proving that the Indian Ocean could be reached by sea. The stage was set for the two voyages that would make the empire: one east to India, one west to the Americas. In 1494 the Treaty of Tordesillas split the newly reachable world between Portugal and Spain along a line in the Atlantic."},{"heading":"The voyages and their captains","text":"Vasco da Gama sailed from Lisbon in 1497 and reached Calicut, on the southwestern coast of India, in 1498 - the first European to arrive there by sea. The route he opened let Portugal trade directly for pepper and other spices, enriching the crown enormously and founding a chain of fortified ports from East Africa to Asia. Two years later, in 1500, Pedro Alvares Cabral led a large fleet bound for India but swung wide into the Atlantic and made landfall on the coast of present-day Brazil, which Portugal then claimed. Whether the detour was accident or design is still debated. These captains built on Henry''s groundwork and Dias''s rounding of the Cape. Within a generation Portugal held a maritime empire stretching across three oceans, trading in spices, sugar, gold, and - over the centuries that followed - millions of enslaved Africans shipped to Brazil. The Discoveries thus brought extraordinary wealth and the beginnings of a global language, but also conquest, forced labor, and upheaval for the peoples whose lands and lives were seized."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Who sponsored early Portuguese exploration from Sagres? 2) Which captain first reached India by sea, and in what year? 3) Who is credited with reaching Brazil in 1500? 4) What did Bartolomeu Dias achieve in 1488? 5) What did the 1494 Treaty of Tordesillas do? Answers: 1) Prince Henry, known as Henry the Navigator. 2) Vasco da Gama, in 1498. 3) Pedro Alvares Cabral. 4) He rounded the Cape of Good Hope, the southern tip of Africa, opening the way to the Indian Ocean. 5) It divided the newly reachable non-European world between Portugal and Spain along a line in the Atlantic."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking Henry the Navigator personally captained long voyages - he organized and funded them from home. Mistake: confusing da Gama''s route to India with Cabral''s landfall in Brazil; one went east around Africa, the other west across the Atlantic. Mistake: describing the era only as heroic adventure, which erases the conquest and the vast trade in enslaved people it set in motion. Exit: (a) Put these in order: Cabral reaches Brazil, Dias rounds the Cape, da Gama reaches India. (b) What was the goal that first drove the voyages? Answers: Dias (1488), da Gama (1498), Cabral (1500); a direct sea route to the spice trade of Asia."}]}'::jsonb, 'The Portuguese term for this era is ''os Descobrimentos'' (the Discoveries), though modern historians stress that the lands reached were already inhabited.', 13),
  (4, 'The Portuguese language across continents', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain where Portuguese comes from, describe how it varies from Lisbon to Rio to Luanda, and recognize why those varieties remain one language. Warm-up: think of a word you use that your grandparents would not, or a phrase that sounds different two towns over. Languages drift like that everywhere, and Portuguese - spread across oceans - drifted a great deal. By the end you should be able to name the family Portuguese belongs to and give a couple of concrete ways Brazilian and European Portuguese differ."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Portuguese is a Romance language, descended from the Latin that Roman soldiers and settlers brought to the Iberian Peninsula. Over centuries the spoken Latin of the northwest, in the region of Galicia and northern Portugal, grew into Galician-Portuguese, and by the medieval period Portuguese had become a distinct literary language. Centuries of contact left their mark: Arabic, from the long Muslim presence in Iberia, gave words like ''azeite'' (olive oil) and ''alface'' (lettuce), many beginning with ''al''. When the language crossed the seas, it kept absorbing. In Brazil it took in words from Indigenous Tupi languages and from the languages of enslaved Africans, which is why so much everyday Brazilian vocabulary for food, plants, and music has roots outside Latin. In Africa, Portuguese sits alongside local languages and Creoles, shaping and being shaped by them. Today it is spoken as a first or official language on four continents, and it is one of the most widely spoken languages in the world - a direct legacy of the voyages studied in the last lesson."},{"heading":"How the varieties differ","text":"European Portuguese, spoken in Portugal, tends to swallow unstressed vowels, giving it a clipped, consonant-heavy sound; Brazilian Portuguese is more open and musical, with fuller vowels that many learners find easier to follow at first. Vocabulary diverges too: a train is a ''comboio'' in Portugal but a ''trem'' in Brazil; a bus is an ''autocarro'' there and an ''onibus'' here. Grammar shows daily differences - Brazilians lean on ''voce'' for ''you'' and often place object pronouns before the verb, while European speakers use ''tu'' widely and attach pronouns to the end. Spelling was partly harmonized by the 1990 Orthographic Agreement, which dropped some silent letters. African varieties add their own accents and borrowings. None of this breaks the language apart: speakers from Porto, Salvador, and Maputo can read one another''s books and, with a little patience, understand one another''s speech. The differences are real and sometimes funny, but they are variations on a shared system, not separate tongues."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What language family does Portuguese belong to? 2) From what older form did it grow in the northwest of Iberia? 3) Name a source of loanwords from the centuries of Muslim presence in Iberia. 4) Give one Brazilian word and its European Portuguese equivalent. 5) Which pronoun for ''you'' is more common in everyday Brazilian speech? Answers: 1) The Romance family, descended from Latin. 2) Galician-Portuguese. 3) Arabic, the source of words such as ''azeite'' and ''alface''. 4) For example ''trem'' (Brazil) versus ''comboio'' (Portugal), or ''onibus'' versus ''autocarro''. 5) ''Voce''."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: calling Brazilian and European Portuguese different languages - they are varieties of one, like British and American English. Mistake: assuming Portuguese is mostly ''just like Spanish''; the two are close relatives but differ sharply in sound and in many common words. Mistake: ignoring the Indigenous and African contributions to Brazilian vocabulary, which are central, not marginal. Exit: (a) Name the two best-known broad varieties of Portuguese. (b) What did the 1990 Orthographic Agreement try to do? Answers: European (Portugal) and Brazilian Portuguese; it standardized spelling across Lusophone countries, dropping some silent letters."}]}'::jsonb, 'European and Brazilian Portuguese are one language with notable differences in pronunciation, vocabulary, and the everyday use of pronouns such as ''tu'' and ''voce''.', 12),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The word ''Lusophone'' derives from Lusitania, the Roman name for the land that became…","options":["Spain","Portugal","Brazil","Angola"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lusitania covered much of modern Portugal, so ''Lusophone'' means Portuguese-speaking."},{"prompt":"Which country is home to the great majority of the world''s Portuguese speakers?","options":["Portugal","Brazil","Mozambique","Angola"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Brazil''s more than 200 million speakers make up over four fifths of the total."},{"prompt":"Bartolomeu Dias''s 1488 achievement was…","options":["reaching India by sea","landing in Brazil","rounding the Cape of Good Hope","sailing around the world"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rounding Africa''s southern tip opened the sea path toward the Indian Ocean."},{"prompt":"The 1494 Treaty of Tordesillas divided the newly reachable world between Portugal and…","options":["France","England","Spain","the Netherlands"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The treaty drew a line in the Atlantic splitting claims between Portugal and Spain."},{"prompt":"Portuguese is a member of which language family?","options":["Germanic","Slavic","Romance","Celtic"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It descends from Latin, making it a Romance language alongside Spanish, French, and Italian."},{"prompt":"In Brazil the common word for ''bus'' is ''onibus''; in Portugal it is…","options":["trem","autocarro","comboio","metro"],"answer":1,"explanation":"European Portuguese uses ''autocarro''; ''comboio'' is the Portuguese word for train."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'Literature: Camoes, Pessoa, Machado de Assis, Jorge Amado', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Match four major writers to their signature works and to the places and eras they wrote in, and explain why Portuguese-language literature spans two very different national traditions. Warm-up: can a single epic poem come to stand for a whole nation''s self-image? Portugal has one, and we start there. By the end you should be able to name the Portuguese national epic and its author, describe what made Pessoa unusual, and tell a Portuguese novelist from a Brazilian one in this list."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Literature in Portuguese grew along two main branches: the older European tradition rooted in Portugal, and the vast, inventive literature of Brazil that came into its own in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries. The European branch begins with medieval lyric poetry and reaches its towering monument in the Renaissance, when Luis de Camoes gave Portugal a national epic. Centuries later, in early twentieth-century Lisbon, Fernando Pessoa reinvented what a poet could be. Across the Atlantic, Brazilian literature matured into a world tradition of its own, producing a master of the psychological novel and, later, a chronicler of color and life in the northeast. These writers differ in language texture, setting, and mood, but all belong to one literary family. Reading them together shows how a shared tongue can carry a crusading Renaissance epic, a modernist''s fractured self, a wry nineteenth-century narrator, and a sun-soaked Bahian romance, each utterly distinct yet mutually legible to any Portuguese reader."},{"heading":"Four writers and their works","text":"Luis de Camoes published ''Os Lusiadas'' (The Lusiads) in 1572, an epic in the classical mold that recounts Vasco da Gama''s voyage to India and, through it, celebrates the whole Portuguese nation; it remains the country''s defining poem. Fernando Pessoa (1888-1935) wrote under multiple ''heteronyms'' - invented poets such as Alberto Caeiro, Ricardo Reis, and Alvaro de Campos, each with a separate voice and life story - and left the haunting prose fragments later gathered as ''The Book of Disquiet''. In Brazil, Machado de Assis (1839-1908), grandson of freed slaves and a founder of the Brazilian Academy of Letters, wrote sly, modern novels such as ''Dom Casmurro'' and ''The Posthumous Memoirs of Bras Cubas'', narrated with irony decades ahead of their time. Jorge Amado (1912-2001), from Bahia, filled his novels with the food, music, and social life of northeastern Brazil in warm, popular books like ''Gabriela, Clove and Cinnamon'' and ''Dona Flor and Her Two Husbands'', among the most widely translated in the language."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Who wrote The Lusiads, and what voyage does it retell? 2) What is a heteronym, and which poet is famous for using them? 3) Which of these writers was Brazilian and helped found the Brazilian Academy of Letters? 4) Name a novel by Jorge Amado. 5) In what century did Camoes write? Answers: 1) Luis de Camoes; it retells Vasco da Gama''s sea voyage to India. 2) A fully invented author with a distinct biography and style; Fernando Pessoa. 3) Machado de Assis. 4) ''Gabriela, Clove and Cinnamon'' or ''Dona Flor and Her Two Husbands''. 5) The sixteenth century, publishing it in 1572."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming all great Portuguese-language writers were from Portugal - Machado de Assis and Jorge Amado were Brazilian, and Brazil''s literature is immense. Mistake: treating Pessoa''s heteronyms as simple pen names rather than distinct imagined authors. Mistake: mixing up The Lusiads (an epic poem) with a novel; it is verse in the classical epic tradition. Exit: (a) Which work is Portugal''s national epic, and who wrote it? (b) Which two writers in this lesson were Brazilian? Answers: The Lusiads, by Luis de Camoes; Machado de Assis and Jorge Amado."}]}'::jsonb, 'A ''heteronym'' is more than a pen name: it is a fully imagined author with a distinct biography and style, a device Fernando Pessoa made famous.', 14),
  (7, 'Music: fado, samba, bossa nova, and MPB', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Tell Portugal''s defining song tradition from Brazil''s major popular styles, name a key artist in each, and explain the mood that ties fado to the idea of ''saudade''. Warm-up: think of a song that sounds like the place it comes from. Music carries a culture''s feeling as directly as anything, and the Portuguese-speaking world is unusually rich in it. By the end you should be able to describe fado in a sentence and place samba, bossa nova, and MPB in rough order of appearance."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Portugal and Brazil each gave the world instantly recognizable music, growing from very different roots. In Portugal, the signature sound is ''fado'', an urban song tradition from the nineteenth century, usually mournful, built on a solo voice backed by the pear-shaped ''guitarra portuguesa''. Its emotional core is ''saudade'', that bittersweet longing which runs through so much Portuguese art. Fado is tied above all to Lisbon, with a more scholarly strain in the university city of Coimbra, and it was recognized by UNESCO as intangible cultural heritage in 2011. Brazil''s traditions are sunnier and more rhythmic, shaped powerfully by African heritage: ''samba'', born in early twentieth-century Rio de Janeiro, became the pulse of Carnival and a national symbol. From samba grew newer styles across the decades. Where fado turns inward toward longing, Brazilian popular music more often turns outward toward dance, celebration, and social commentary - though both can hold deep feeling. Together they show two faces of the same Lusophone musical family."},{"heading":"Styles and voices","text":"Fado''s most celebrated voice was Amalia Rodrigues, who carried the tradition worldwide in the twentieth century and remains its defining figure. In Brazil, samba''s roots lie in Afro-Brazilian communities of Rio, and it anchors the parades of Carnival. In the late 1950s a quieter, jazz-influenced offshoot appeared: ''bossa nova'', with its soft rhythms and understated singing, created by figures such as the composer Antonio Carlos Jobim (Tom Jobim), the guitarist and singer Joao Gilberto, and the poet Vinicius de Moraes; their song ''The Girl from Ipanema'' became a global standard. From the 1960s onward, ''MPB'' - Musica Popular Brasileira - gathered sophisticated songwriters who blended samba, bossa nova, folk, and rock, often with poetic or political lyrics; its leading names include Caetano Veloso, Gilberto Gil, Chico Buarque, and the beloved singer Elis Regina. So the rough timeline runs: fado maturing in nineteenth-century Portugal, samba in early twentieth-century Brazil, bossa nova from the late 1950s, and MPB from the 1960s on."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What is fado, and which emotion is central to it? 2) Which instrument typically accompanies fado? 3) Who was the most celebrated fado singer? 4) Name two of the figures behind bossa nova. 5) What does MPB stand for? 6) Which style is the pulse of Brazilian Carnival? Answers: 1) A mournful Portuguese urban song; saudade, a bittersweet longing. 2) The guitarra portuguesa. 3) Amalia Rodrigues. 4) Any two of Antonio Carlos Jobim, Joao Gilberto, and Vinicius de Moraes. 5) Musica Popular Brasileira. 6) Samba."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: calling fado a dance - it is a sung tradition, usually slow and reflective. Mistake: treating samba, bossa nova, and MPB as the same thing; they are related but distinct, appearing in that rough order. Mistake: assuming ''saudade'' just means sadness, when it is longing for something absent, a mix of sweet and sorrowful. Exit: (a) From which country does fado come, and which Brazilian style grew out of samba in the late 1950s? (b) Name one MPB artist. Answers: Fado is Portuguese; bossa nova grew from samba in the late 1950s; an MPB artist such as Caetano Veloso, Gilberto Gil, Chico Buarque, or Elis Regina."}]}'::jsonb, '''Saudade'' is a key Portuguese word often said to have no exact English match: a deep, bittersweet longing for something or someone absent.', 13),
  (8, 'Art, azulejos, food, and daily life', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe what azulejos are and where they came from, name signature dishes of Portugal and Brazil, and picture how food and craft shape daily life in both countries. Warm-up: what do you think a nation that lives by the sea and once sailed the world might eat most? Keep salted fish in mind. By the end you should be able to explain the origin of the word ''azulejo'' and name at least two dishes from each country, with a sense of the everyday rhythms around them."},{"heading":"Art and azulejos","text":"Portugal''s most distinctive visual art is the ''azulejo'', the painted, glazed ceramic tile that covers the walls of churches, palaces, ordinary houses, and even train stations. The technique arrived through contact with the Muslim world - the very word comes from Arabic - and over centuries the Portuguese made it their own, especially the blue-and-white panels that became iconic from the 1600s on. Whole walls tell stories in tile: saints'' lives, sea battles, scenes of harvest and trade. The hall of the Sao Bento railway station in Porto, lined with tiles, is a famous example. Beyond azulejos, Portugal has a rich tradition of Manueline architecture, an ornate style from the Discoveries era studded with ropes, knots, and sea motifs. Brazil developed its own strands, from ornate Baroque churches in Ouro Preto, carved by the sculptor known as Aleijadinho, to twentieth-century modernism, crowned by the architect Oscar Niemeyer''s sweeping curves in the planned capital, Brasilia. Across the Lusophone world, art often lives in public space - on walls, in squares, along station platforms - rather than only in museums."},{"heading":"Food and daily life","text":"Portuguese cooking leans on the Atlantic. ''Bacalhau'', dried salted cod, is a national obsession, said to have a different preparation for every day of the year; grilled sardines scent the summer streets; ''caldo verde'', a kale-and-potato soup, warms the winter. Meals end with the custard tart ''pastel de nata'' and, for celebration, a glass of port wine from the Douro valley. Daily life keeps a Mediterranean rhythm: a long midday meal, strong coffee, and evenings in the cafe. Brazil''s table is broader and tropical. ''Feijoada'', a hearty black-bean stew with pork, is the classic weekend gathering dish; cheese bread called ''pao de queijo'', grilled ''churrasco'' meats, the Amazonian berry ''acai'', and the sweet ''brigadeiro'' all feature, often washed down with a ''caipirinha''. In both countries, eating is social and unhurried, family-centered, and tied to the calendar of feasts and seasons - a reminder that culture lives as much at the table as in any gallery or concert hall."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What are azulejos, and from which language does the word come? 2) Name the salted fish central to Portuguese cooking. 3) Which Portuguese custard tart is famous worldwide? 4) Name the classic Brazilian black-bean stew. 5) Which architect designed much of Brasilia? Answers: 1) Painted glazed ceramic tiles; the word comes from Arabic (''az-zulayj''). 2) Bacalhau, dried salted cod. 3) The pastel de nata. 4) Feijoada. 5) Oscar Niemeyer."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking azulejos are purely decorative when many narrate history and religion across entire walls. Mistake: assuming Portuguese and Brazilian food are the same - both love hearty, social meals, but the Atlantic larder of Portugal differs from Brazil''s tropical one. Mistake: treating the word ''azulejo'' as related to ''azul'' (blue) by coincidence; the tiles are often blue, but the name comes from Arabic for a polished stone. Exit: (a) Where might you expect to see azulejos in a Portuguese city? (b) Name one signature dish from each country. Answers: On churches, palaces, houses, and train stations such as Sao Bento in Porto; for example bacalhau or pastel de nata in Portugal, and feijoada or pao de queijo in Brazil."}]}'::jsonb, 'The word ''azulejo'' comes from the Arabic ''az-zulayj'', meaning a polished stone, a reminder of Iberia''s long Muslim heritage.', 13),
  (9, 'Festivals, religion, society, and the colonial legacy', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe major festivals in Portugal and Brazil, outline the religious landscape of both, and explain in plain terms how the colonial past shapes the societies of today. Warm-up: how can a street party and a church calendar be two sides of the same tradition? Many Lusophone festivals began as saints'' days and grew into vast popular celebrations. By the end you should be able to name a festival in each country and give one clear example of how colonial history still echoes in modern Portugal and Brazil."},{"heading":"Festivals","text":"Brazil''s ''Carnaval'' is among the most famous festivals on earth: in the days before the Christian season of Lent, cities erupt in parades, music, and dance. Rio de Janeiro is known for its glittering samba-school parades in a purpose-built avenue, while Salvador, in Bahia, throws an enormous street party led by trucks mounted with bands. In June, ''Festas Juninas'' fill the countryside with bonfires, folk dances, and foods of corn, honoring saints of the harvest. Portugal has its own calendar. The ''Festas de Lisboa'' light up June around the feast of Saint Anthony (Santo Antonio), the city''s patron: neighborhoods grill sardines by the ton, string up paper decorations, and hold the ''marchas populares'', costumed neighborhood parades through the old districts. Porto celebrates Saint John in a similar spirit. Across both nations, these festivals mix a Catholic framework with much older popular custom - and, in Brazil especially, with African-rooted rhythm and ritual - turning the religious calendar into a shared public joy."},{"heading":"Religion, society, and the colonial legacy","text":"Both countries were deeply shaped by Roman Catholicism, carried everywhere by Portuguese colonization; Brazil long held the title of the world''s largest Catholic nation, and the Portuguese shrine at Fatima, site of reported apparitions in 1917, draws pilgrims from around the globe. Yet the picture is changing: Brazil has seen rapid growth of Evangelical Protestant churches, and Afro-Brazilian religions such as Candomble and Umbanda, rooted in the faiths of enslaved Africans, remain vital. That last fact points to the hardest part of this history. Portugal built a seaborne empire that trafficked millions of enslaved Africans, above all to Brazil, which received more than any other country and abolished slavery only in 1888. Brazil became independent in 1822; most of the African colonies won freedom in 1975, after Portugal''s own Carnation Revolution of 1974 ended a long dictatorship. Today Portugal is a democratic member of the European Union, which it joined in 1986, while Brazil is the largest economy in South America. Both remain marked - in language, faith, music, and society - by this entwined and painful past."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name the pre-Lenten festival for which Brazil is famous. 2) Which saint''s feast anchors the Festas de Lisboa, and in which month? 3) Name one Afro-Brazilian religion. 4) In what year did Brazil become independent? 5) What 1974 event helped end Portugal''s dictatorship and led to African decolonization? 6) When did Portugal join the European Union? Answers: 1) Carnaval (Carnival). 2) Saint Anthony (Santo Antonio), in June. 3) Candomble or Umbanda. 4) 1822. 5) The Carnation Revolution. 6) 1986."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming the whole Lusophone world is uniformly Catholic today, when Evangelical churches and Afro-Brazilian religions are major forces, especially in Brazil. Mistake: picturing festivals as purely religious or purely secular; most blend the two. Mistake: discussing the empire''s wealth without its human cost - the trade in enslaved people and its long aftermath. Exit: (a) Give one festival from Portugal and one from Brazil. (b) Name one lasting effect of the colonial era on modern Brazilian or Portuguese society. Answers: For example the Festas de Lisboa (Portugal) and Carnaval (Brazil); lasting effects include the dominance of the Portuguese language, Catholic tradition, and - from the slave trade - the deep African roots of Brazilian religion, food, and music."}]}'::jsonb, 'Brazil''s ''Carnaval'' and Portugal''s ''Festas de Lisboa'' both fuse Catholic calendars with older popular and, in Brazil, African-rooted traditions.', 14),
  (10, 'Checkpoint 2', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Fernando Pessoa is especially known for writing under…","options":["a single lifelong pen name","multiple heteronyms, each an invented author","his brother''s name","no name at all"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pessoa created heteronyms such as Alberto Caeiro and Alvaro de Campos, each a distinct imagined poet."},{"prompt":"Which Brazilian style grew out of samba and jazz in the late 1950s?","options":["fado","bossa nova","MPB","Festas Juninas"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bossa nova, with figures like Jobim and Joao Gilberto, emerged in Rio in the late 1950s."},{"prompt":"The word ''azulejo'' comes from…","options":["Latin for blue","Arabic for a polished stone","Tupi for clay","Greek for tile"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It derives from the Arabic ''az-zulayj'', a legacy of Iberia''s Muslim past."},{"prompt":"Feijoada, pao de queijo, and brigadeiro are dishes from…","options":["Portugal","Brazil","Angola","Cape Verde"],"answer":1,"explanation":"These are classic Brazilian foods; Portugal''s staples include bacalhau and the pastel de nata."},{"prompt":"The Festas de Lisboa in June center on the feast of…","options":["Saint John","Saint Anthony","Saint Peter","Our Lady of Fatima"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lisbon celebrates its patron, Santo Antonio, with sardines and the marchas populares."},{"prompt":"Brazil became independent from Portugal in…","options":["1750","1822","1888","1975"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Brazil declared independence in 1822; slavery there was abolished later, in 1888."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
